---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ArticleFrame from '../../../components/ArticleFrame.astro';
const toc=[{id:'shape',label:'Circle or star'},{id:'position',label:'Move, resize, rotate'},{id:'batch',label:'Batch mode'},{id:'review',label:'Review results'},{id:'scope',label:'Visible pixels only'}];
const related=[{href:'/gemini-ai-logo-remover/',label:'Open Gemini AI Logo Remover'},{href:'/remove-watermark/',label:'General Remove Watermark'},{href:'/guides/how-to-remove-watermark-from-image/',label:'Watermark removal guide'}];
---
<SiteLayout title="How to Remove a Visible Gemini AI Logo from an Image | ConvertAnyFile" description="Learn the focused Circle and 4-Point-Star workflow for moving, resizing and rotating a small visible Gemini-style badge locally, including shared and individual batch masks.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Gemini AI Logo</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Image Editing Guide</span><h1>How to remove a visible Gemini-style AI logo</h1><p>Use a focused Circle or 4-Point-Star mask, position and rotate it over the visible badge, choose the right batch behavior, and review the local repair before download.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} asideNote="ConvertAnyFile is not affiliated with or endorsed by Google or Gemini.">
<h2 id="shape">1. Start with 4-Point Star or Circle</h2><p>Open <a href="/gemini-ai-logo-remover/">Gemini AI Logo Remover</a>. The toolbar intentionally contains only <strong>Circle</strong> and <strong>4-Point Star</strong>. The star is the default because it follows the general footprint of a small sparkle-style badge. Circle is useful when the visible glow is more rounded.</p>
<h2 id="position">2. Move, resize, and rotate the selected mask</h2><p>Drag inside the selected mask to move it, use a square corner handle to resize, or use the round handle to rotate it when the visible badge is tilted. Keep the selection as tight as practical while still covering the visible badge.</p>
<h2 id="batch">3. Let image layout choose a safe starting mode</h2><p>When the images share an aspect ratio, the editor starts with One mask for all. When mixed aspect ratios are detected, it starts with Edit individually. You can override the recommendation at any time.</p><p>For a fast repeated workflow, place one shared mask first. If only a few images differ in position, size, or angle, switch to individual editing and customize those exceptions.</p>
<h2 id="review">4. Repair locally and inspect each result</h2><p>The browser processes full-resolution decoded pixels sequentially and can package multiple successful results into a ZIP. Click completed thumbnails to restore their After preview. Smooth or repeated backgrounds are generally easier than faces, text, hair, or sharp structural edges.</p>
<h2 id="scope">5. Understand the scope</h2><p>This workflow edits a visible selected badge. It does not claim to identify or remove invisible watermark systems, Content Credentials, provenance metadata, authenticity signals, or SynthID-style signals. Keep source files when provenance or archival history matters.</p>
</ArticleFrame>
</SiteLayout>
